---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [{
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: 'Can a team that does not write Rust still use Traverse?',
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: "Yes. Consume capabilities with no Rust (npm embedder or CLI). Author new ones in plain English via the official Claude skill traverse-capability-author — under the hood that still emits Rust→WASM. Manual authoring in the repo still expects Rust. There is no shipped Python SDK; Python consumes via traverse-cli subprocess today.",
    },
  }],
});

const relatedLinks = [
  { href: '/questions/do-i-need-rust.html', label: 'Do I need to know Rust to use Traverse?' },
  { href: '/questions/can-i-use-traverse-with-python.html', label: 'Can I use Traverse with Python?' },
  { href: '/docs/guides/react-integration.html', label: 'React Integration guide' },
  { href: '/questions/what-is-traverse-roadmap.html', label: 'What is the Traverse roadmap?' },
];
---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Can a team that doesn't write Rust still use Traverse?"
  description="Yes: consume with no Rust, or author in plain English via traverse-capability-author. Manual authoring still expects Rust. No Python SDK — CLI subprocess today."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/can-non-rust-teams-use-traverse.html"
  category="Question"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>Depends whether your team is consuming capabilities or authoring them, and those have very different Rust requirements.</p>

  <p><strong>Consuming a capability</strong> requires no Rust whatsoever. If you're calling into a capability from a React app, you're sending a plain JSON request to the local browser adapter and reading a JSON response back — the <a href="/docs/guides/react-integration.html">React integration guide</a> covers this in full and never touches Rust or WASM directly. The same is true from any language that can make an HTTP request or bind against an embedder's API.</p>

  <p><strong>Authoring a new capability</strong> does not require your team to write Rust either, if you use the official Claude skill <code>traverse-capability-author</code> in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/claude-skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traverse-framework/claude-skills</a>. You describe the rule in plain English; the skill checks the registry, writes the contract, produces executable WASM, and opens a human-reviewed PR. Under the hood that implementation is still Rust→WASM (determinism, sandbox, portability). The manual path — writing the capability yourself in the repo — still expects Rust.</p>

  <p>There is no shipped Python SDK. Python consumes today by shelling out to <code>traverse-cli capability-package execute</code> (stdlib <code>subprocess</code>). See <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1362" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traverse#1362</a>.</p>

  <h2>A practical split for a mixed team</h2>
  <p>A common shape: product or domain people describe capabilities through the skill (or a small Rust-comfortable group maintains the manual path), while the rest of the team — frontend, data, ML — consumes them as a plain API. That matches how the contract boundary is meant to work. The contract is the interface a non-Rust consumer needs to understand; the Rust→WASM implementation behind it is an internal detail they don't have to touch.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
